WS holds approximately 29–30% of the global cloud infrastructure market, Azure around 20%, while GCP consistently remains in third place with a 12–13% share. **Google, having started later,** still has fewer regions (around 40 versus 60+ for Azure and 30+ for AWS with a much larger number of availability zones), which is critical for large global customers with requirements for low latency, data localization, and regulatory compliance. **As a result, Azure wins large multi-year contracts in traditional corporations**, while GCP is more often chosen by technology companies, startups, or projects where data analytics, AI/ML, and Kubernetes are critical (BigQuery, Vertex AI, GKE). In artificial intelligence, the situation looks different: GCP leads thanks to TPU and Google’s deep expertise, AWS wins through ecosystem breadth and tool diversity, and Azure through integration with enterprise products and the OpenAI stack.", "score": 0.9563089, "raw_content": null}, {"url": "https://www.globaldots.com/resources/blog/aws-vs-azure-vs-gcp-the-ups-and-downs-of-the-public-cloud-trifecta/", "title": "AWS vs Azure vs GCP – The Ups And Downs Of The Public Cloud ...", "content": "Home   Resources   Blog   AWS vs Azure vs GCP – The Ups And Downs Of The Public Cloud Trifecta. # AWS vs Azure vs GCP – The Ups And Downs Of The Public Cloud Trifecta. As the title suggest, Amazon Web Services (AWS), Microsoft’s Azure and Google Cloud Platform (GCP) are the leaders that provide the **most reliable, functional and safest cloud services**. Logos of Google Cloud Platform, Amazon Web Services, and Microsoft Azure with a lock. “*Amazon Web Services is a secure cloud services platform, offering compute power, database storage, content delivery and other functionality to help businesses scale and grow*. Microsoft’s own description of Azure, AWS’ **major competitor** in the public cloud market:. When it seemed that AWS and Azure were the only serious market contenders, Google’s management decided